steps.message:
This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
1. Primary Request and Intent:
   The user filed a bug report (no additional chat instructions beyond the report itself) titled "Context menu misbehaves when right-clicked again while it is already open." Full text of the report:

   > I am wrapping an element with a context menu. When the menu is already open and I right-click the element again in a different place, it does not respond the way I would expect. The recording below shows what happens on that second right-click.
   > ![Context menu on a second right-click while it is already open](/app/.harbor_assets/bug.gif)

   The implicit task is to investigate this repo (Radix UI Primitives monorepo) to find the root cause of the bug and (implicitly, as the next logical step, though not yet explicitly confirmed by the user) propose/implement a fix. No further clarifying instructions have been given by the user yet since this is the first exchange.

2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - Radix UI Primitives monorepo (pnpm workspace, package name "primitives")
   - `@radix-ui/react-context-menu` built on top of `@radix-ui/react-menu`, which itself builds on `@radix-ui/react-popper`, `@radix-ui/react-dismissable-layer`, `@radix-ui/react-focus-scope`, `@radix-ui/react-roving-focus`
   - `useControllableState` for open/close state management (a no-op setState call when value is unchanged — i.e., calling `onOpenChange(true)` while already `true` doesn't cause a state change or re-render)
   - Virtual reference pattern (`virtualRef` with `getBoundingClientRect()`) used to position the context menu at the mouse coordinates instead of a real DOM anchor element
   - `@floating-ui/react-dom`'s `useFloating`/`autoUpdate` for popper positioning; position recalculation is driven by resize/scroll/mutation observers, not by arbitrary JS mutations to a ref's closure state
   - `DismissableLayer`'s `disableOutsidePointerEvents` behavior: sets `document.body.style.pointerEvents = 'none'` while a modal layer (default `modal=true` for ContextMenu) is open, and layers stack via `context.layersWithOutsidePointerEventsDisabled`
   - React `useRef` identity stability — an object created once via `useRef({...})` never changes reference across renders, which affects reference-equality checks like `previousAnchor !== anchorRef.current`
   - Native browser `contextmenu` event and `event.preventDefault()` — needed to suppress the native OS/browser context menu when the custom Radix menu should show instead
   - Testing stack: Vitest + Testing Library (`fireEvent.contextMenu`, `screen`, `waitFor`) for unit tests in `context-menu.test.tsx`; Cypress e2e tests in `cypress/e2e/ContextMenu.cy.ts` using `cy.rightclick()`
   - AGENTS.md conventions: pnpm only, TypeScript only, Conventional Commits, braces for control flow always, exhaustive `never` checks, pre-commit checklist (`pnpm lint --fix`, `pnpm format`), PR checklist (`pnpm format:check`, `pnpm lint`, `pnpm test`, `pnpm types:check`)

3. Files and Code Sections:
   - `/app/.harbor_assets/bug.gif`
     - The bug report recording. Read directly and also decomposed via `convert -coalesce` into 40 PNG frames at `/tmp/gif_frames/frame_000.png`...`frame_039.png` (using ImageMagick's `convert`, since Python's PIL was not installed and no ffmpeg/gifsicle available).
     - Frame durations (centiseconds) via `identify -format "%f %T\n"`: mostly 15 (150ms), with a few longer holds (45, 30, 75) at specific frames (index 1→45, index 14→30, near end 35→45, 36→75, 38→30).
     - Visual narrative observed across frames:
       - Frames 0–4: A native browser (Firefox-style) context menu is open at one position over the "Zahra Ambessa" row (menu items: Back, Forward, Reload, Bookmark Page…, Save Page As…, Save Page to Pocket, Select All, This Frame ▸, View Page Source, Inspect Accessibility Properties, Inspect, Redux DevTools ▸).
       - Frames 5–13: A DIFFERENT, app-style custom menu is now shown instead (items: Edit ⌘E, Duplicate ⌘D, Archive ⌘N, More ▸, Share, Add to favorites, Delete ⌘⌫), positioned further right/up, overlapping the "Group" column — this is presumably the demo/Radix ContextMenu content.
       - Frames 14–24: This custom menu persists in the same position while the mouse cursor moves to various points nearby (indicating it did NOT reposition/reopen in response to subsequent right-clicks at new locations).
       - Frames 25–34: The native browser context menu (Back/Forward/Reload…) reappears at a third, different position.
       - Frames 35–39: All menus disappear; clean table view remains.
     - This visual sequence is consistent with the hypothesis: after the first right-click opens the custom Radix context menu, a second right-click at a different location fails to reposition/reopen the Radix menu and instead the browser's native context menu appears (implying `event.preventDefault()` was never called on that second `contextmenu` event).

   - `/app/packages/react/context-menu/src/context-menu.tsx` (624 lines) — the central file, likely needing a fix. Full `ContextMenuTrigger` implementation read (lines 99–188):
     ```jsx
     const ContextMenuTrigger = React.forwardRef<ContextMenuTriggerElement, ContextMenuTriggerProps>(
       (props: ScopedProps<ContextMenuTriggerProps>, forwardedRef) => {
         const { __scopeContextMenu, disabled = false, ...triggerProps } = props;
         const context = useContextMenuContext(TRIGGER_NAME, __scopeContextMenu);
         const menuScope = useMenuScope(__scopeContextMenu);
         const pointRef = React.useRef<Point>({ x: 0, y: 0 });
         const virtualRef = React.useRef({
           getBoundingClientRect: () => DOMRect.fromRect({ width: 0, height: 0, ...pointRef.current }),
         });
         const longPressTimerRef = React.useRef(0);
         const clearLongPress = React.useCallback(
           () => window.clearTimeout(longPressTimerRef.current),
           [],
         );
         const handleOpen = (event: React.MouseEvent | React.PointerEvent) => {
           context.hasInteractedRef.current = true;
           pointRef.current = { x: event.clientX, y: event.clientY };
           context.onOpenChange(true);
         };

         React.useEffect(() => clearLongPress, [clearLongPress]);
         React.useEffect(() => void (disabled && clearLongPress()), [disabled, clearLongPress]);

         return (
           <>
             <MenuPrimitive.Anchor {...menuScope} virtualRef={virtualRef} />
             <Primitive.span
               data-state={context.open ? 'open' : 'closed'}
               data-disabled={disabled ? '' : undefined}
               {...triggerProps}
               ref={forwardedRef}
               style={{ WebkitTouchCallout: 'none', ...props.style }}
               onContextMenu={
                 disabled
                   ? props.onContextMenu
                   : composeEventHandlers(props.onContextMenu, (event) => {
                       clearLongPress();
                       handleOpen(event);
                       event.preventDefault();
                     })
               }
               onPointerDown={
                 disabled
                   ? props.onPointerDown
                   : composeEventHandlers(
                       props.onPointerDown,
                       whenTouchOrPen((event) => {
                         clearLongPress();
                         if (context.open) {
                           context.onOpenChange(false);
                         }
                         longPressTimerRef.current = window.setTimeout(() => handleOpen(event), 700);
                       }),
                     )
               }
               onPointerMove={ ... whenTouchOrPen(clearLongPress) }
               onPointerCancel={ ... whenTouchOrPen(clearLongPress) }
               onPointerUp={ ... whenTouchOrPen(clearLongPress) }
             />
           </>
         );
       },
     );
     ```
     Note: for touch/pen (`onPointerDown`), there IS explicit handling to close-then-reopen (`if (context.open) { context.onOpenChange(false); }` before scheduling the long-press reopen) — but the mouse-driven `onContextMenu` handler has NO such close-then-reopen logic; it just calls `handleOpen` unconditionally, which calls `context.onOpenChange(true)` — a no-op if already `true` due to `useControllableState`.

     Also reviewed the `ContextMenu` root component (lines 43–91), which wires up `useControllableState`, warns in dev if `open` prop is set true before user interaction, and renders `MenuPrimitive.Root` with `modal = true` by default.

     Also reviewed `ContextMenuContent` (lines 222–267) which sets `side="right"`, `sideOffset={2}`, `align="start"` and handles `onCloseAutoFocus`/`onInteractOutside` for non-modal focus-outside handling.

   - `/app/packages/react/context-menu/src/context-menu.test.tsx` — read substantial portions (lines 1–120, 300–470). Contains a `ContextMenuTest` helper component wrapping `ContextMenu.Root`/`Trigger`/`Portal`/`Content`/`Item`. Test suites: "rendering and opening", "disabled trigger", "items", submenu-related tests (aria-controls, disabled submenu), "modality" (checks `document.body.style.pointerEvents` is `'none'` while modal menu open and reset to `''` on Escape; non-modal menu doesn't disable body pointer-events), and "controlled `open` state" (checks `onOpenChange` calls, controlled `open={false}` blocking, programmatic close, Escape-triggered `onOpenChange(false)`). No existing test currently covers "right-click again while already open at a different position" — this would need to be added.
     Relevant modality test:
     ```jsx
     it('disables pointer events on the body while a modal menu is open', async () => {
       render(<ContextMenuTest />);
       expect(document.body.style.pointerEvents).toBe('');
       fireEvent.contextMenu(screen.getByText(TRIGGER_TEXT));
       await waitFor(() => expect(document.body.style.pointerEvents).toBe('none'));
       fireEvent.keyDown(document.activeElement!, { key: 'Escape' });
       await waitFor(() => expect(document.body.style.pointerEvents).toBe(''));
     });
     ```
     This confirms modal ContextMenu sets `body.style.pointerEvents = 'none'` while open — supporting the hypothesis that a second right-click on the trigger (or anywhere) while the menu is open may be blocked from reaching the trigger's `onContextMenu` handler due to this CSS, causing the native browser context menu to appear instead (since `preventDefault()` never executes).

   - `/app/packages/react/menu/src/menu.tsx` (1443 lines) — fully read. Key relevant pieces:
     - `Menu` root component sets up `PopperPrimitive.Root`, tracks `isUsingKeyboardRef`, and (via a comment referencing issue #3257) closes the menu on window `blur`.
     - `MenuContentImpl` wires `DismissableLayer`, `FocusScope`, `RovingFocusGroup.Root`, and `PopperPrimitive.Content`, forwarding `disableOutsidePointerEvents`, `onDismiss`, etc.
     - `MenuRootContentModal` sets `disableOutsidePointerEvents={context.open}` and `trapFocus={context.open}`.
     - Comment "Prevent the parent menu from reopening with open submenus" in `MenuSub` (unrelated but noted while grepping for "reopen").

   - `/app/packages/react/dismissable-layer/src/dismissable-layer.tsx` (556 lines) — fully read. Key relevant piece — the effect that manages the shared `body.style.pointerEvents`:
     ```jsx
     React.useEffect(() => {
       if (!node) return;
       if (disableOutsidePointerEvents) {
         if (context.layersWithOutsidePointerEventsDisabled.size === 0) {
           originalBodyPointerEvents = ownerDocument.body.style.pointerEvents;
           ownerDocument.body.style.pointerEvents = 'none';
         }
         context.layersWithOutsidePointerEventsDisabled.add(node);
       }
       context.layers.add(node);
       dispatchUpdate();
       return () => {
         if (disableOutsidePointerEvents) {
           context.layersWithOutsidePointerEventsDisabled.delete(node);
           if (context.layersWithOutsidePointerEventsDisabled.size === 0) {
             ownerDocument.body.style.pointerEvents = originalBodyPointerEvents;
           }
         }
       };
     }, [node, ownerDocument, disableOutsidePointerEvents, context]);
     ```
     Also the `DismissableLayer`'s own rendered element applies `pointerEvents: isPointerEventsEnabled ? 'auto' : 'none'` on itself when body pointer-events are globally disabled, to re-enable interaction just for the topmost/permitted layer(s). This means the layer's own subtree (the `MenuContent`) can still receive pointer events (via inline style `pointer-events: auto` override), but anything OUTSIDE any tracked layer/branch (like the raw trigger `<span>`, which is NOT a `DismissableLayerBranch` and not itself a layer) is still covered by `body { pointer-events: none }` and thus cannot receive pointer/contextmenu interaction — this is the crux of the suspected bug.
     Also noted `DismissableLayerBranch` / `useDismissableLayerSurface` exports, used elsewhere only by `dialog.tsx` (via grep) — not currently used by `context-menu.tsx` or `menu.tsx` for the trigger element.

   - `/app/packages/react/popper/src/popper.tsx` (489 lines) — fully read. Key relevant piece — `PopperAnchor`'s virtual-ref handling effect:
     ```jsx
     const anchorRef = React.useRef<Measurable | null>(null);
     React.useEffect(() => {
       if (!virtualRef) {
         return;
       }
       const previousAnchor = anchorRef.current;
       anchorRef.current = virtualRef.current;
       if (previousAnchor !== anchorRef.current) {
         onAnchorChange(anchorRef.current);
       }
     });
     ```
     This runs on every render (no dependency array) but compares `virtualRef.current` (a stable object identity, since `virtualRef` itself is a `useRef({...})` created once in `ContextMenuTrigger`) against the previously stored value. Because the object identity never changes across renders, `onAnchorChange` fires only ONCE ever (the first time `anchorRef.current` transitions from `null` to the virtualRef object) and never again — even though `pointRef.current` (read inside `getBoundingClientRect()`) is mutated on every right-click. This means `context.anchor` in Popper's context is set once and never "changes" from React's perspective, which likely prevents floating-ui's `useFloating`/`autoUpdate` from recognizing that a reposition is needed when the user right-clicks again at a new location while the menu is already open (since nothing signals a change: no resize, no scroll, no DOM mutation, no anchor object identity change).
     Also read `PopperContent`'s `useFloftware `useFloating` call: `elements: { reference: context.anchor }`, with `whileElementsMounted: autoUpdate(...)`.

   - `/app/apps/ssr-testing/app/context-menu/page.tsx` — simple demo page using `ContextMenu.Root`/`Trigger`/`Portal`/`Content` with Undo/Redo/Cut/Copy/Paste items; reviewed for context but not modified.

   - `/app/packages/react/dropdown-menu/src/dropdown-menu.tsx` — partially read (lines 1–140) for comparison; noted `DropdownMenuTrigger`'s `onPointerDown` pattern using `composeEventHandlers` and explicit `event.button === 0` / `ctrlKey` checks — not directly used for the fix but examined as a sibling primitive pattern.

   - CHANGELOG.md files for `context-menu`, `dismissable-layer`, and `menu` packages — skimmed; no existing changelog entry matches this exact bug (nothing about "right click again while open" or reposition-on-reopen). Confirms this would be a new fix requiring a new changeset/changelog entry per repo conventions.

   - `/app/AGENTS.md` — read in full; documents repo conventions (pnpm/TypeScript only, Conventional Commits, code conventions favoring braces and explicit `never` checks, pre-commit and PR checklists requiring `pnpm lint --fix`, `pnpm format`, `pnpm format:check`, `pnpm lint`, `pnpm test`, `pnpm types:check`).

   - No files have been edited yet. No tests have been run yet. This has been a pure read/investigation phase.

4. Errors and fixes:
   - PIL/Pillow not installed (`ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'PIL'`) when trying to inspect the GIF programmatically via Python — worked around by using the already-available `convert` (ImageMagick) CLI tool instead (`ffmpeg`/`gifsicle` were not installed either).
   - `npx playwright --version` failed because Playwright wasn't installed locally and `npx` couldn't install it without confirmation (`npm error npx canceled due to missing packages and no YES option: ["playwright@1.62.1"]`) — this avenue (browser-driving to reproduce live) was abandoned in favor of static code reading and GIF frame analysis.
   - No user feedback has been received yet correcting my approach — this is still the first response cycle after the initial bug report, no back-and-forth confirmation happened before the compaction request arrived.

5. Problem Solving:
   - Established strong circumstantial evidence for two compounding root causes:
     (a) `ContextMenuTrigger`'s `onContextMenu` handler (mouse path) unconditionally calls `context.onOpenChange(true)` without first closing if already open — unlike the touch/pen `onPointerDown` path, which explicitly does `if (context.open) { context.onOpenChange(false); }` before reopening. Because `useControllableState` no-ops when setting the same value, calling `onOpenChange(true)` while already open does not change the open state and won't force any position/content re-evaluation.
